so good afternoon I mean a hutch Nicki  in the user interface group and today  I'm going to talk briefly about how you  can work with documents on the cloud  specifically focusing on working with  them inside of the desktop products  either Mathematica or wolfram desktop  just as a quick tour we're going to talk  about what's currently their interface  wise we're going to talk briefly about  what kind of notebook functions are  aware of cloud objects and then the real  meat of this talk is actually going to  be about what's coming so much like most  cloud-enabled applications the most  common way that you actually end up  interacting with them is is actually  opening modifying and then saving  content directly back to it and the  simplest form of that of course is the  generic old-fashioned open and save  dialog which we have for the cloud if  you've never seen it it supports login  we have login support in the product and  they're located right there the open and  save dialog or themed to match the the  cloud styling but the but the usage  patterns are the same we don't do a  whole lot of maintenance so there's no  you know like oh I want to rename files  inside of it we're not trying to  replicate finder or explore inside of  our product originally we tried it and  decided that was a little bit of  overkill if you're interested in seeing  that happen in the future I'd be  interested in hearing about that  afterwards but the only thing that is  truly useful is for being able to modify  your cloud storage directly is we give  you the ability to save sorry in the  save dialog we give you the ability to  create a new folder the real interesting  is the activity monitor oh hmm actually  i'll leave that there that's actually  fortuitous the the activity monitor all  the interactions that the front end has  with the cloud happens asynchronously in  a queue so whenever you go to open the  document or save content back to the  cloud it actually happens off of a queue  so it can happen on a somewhat  maintainable schedule without  overloading for instance your own  network connection or you know if  everybody decided to save the exact same  thing at the same time then we don't  take the cloud down with it as well it's  also the place where as you can see it  actually ends up reporting errors so  that way you can actually maintain you  can indicate to the system what you're  trying what you would like to do here  either trying again in this instance or  you can actually abort it I took the  bold step we're going to try it maybe it  was because I turned it off earlier the  the interesting bit about this is is  that on Windows and Linux in version 10  we actually shut the application down  when you've closed all of your windows  but if this is still syncing that would  actually end up in terminating the  process in the middle so you would never  have your data finished uploading so the  cloud activity monitor basically is  there to keep the system going to allow  the data to continue to upload and  finish out otherwise it ends up having a  very odd experience of you thought you  saved it you shut your laptop you come  and use it on a different machine and  then all of a sudden all your data isn't  there anymore so from interface  interface perspective it's pretty  standard from a UI point of view inside  the document we just added a few bit of  error handling messaging it lets you  know that the the front-end knows if you  have active internet connections and it  will let you know that you're in that  it's impossible to save document  because you lack Internet connectivity  it also report errors in that place as  well and we have this cute little bubble  at the bottom just to let you know that  saves are pending oft in the background  as well in case you don't have the  Activity Monitor around but the real  interesting stuff is actually the the  notebook functions in the language that  we can use with cloud objects so the  obvious one of course is notebook open  you know given an arbitrary cloud object  you can actually directly open the  notebook in the front end modify it to  your heart's content you can then  actually call notebook save on that  notebook object and it will then send it  back but as I mentioned it is  asynchronous which means that the data  itself will not be sent to the server by  the time the function is finished that's  something to be aware of and we don't we  didn't have the two argument form in 10  for notebook information we made a few  tweaks along the way we added two new  fields to the result one is basically  storage class which describes whether or  not the notebook is backed locally on  your file system or you're in the cloud  and if its cloud-based object it of  course has a URI field that lets you  know the cloud object that's actually  backing it but that's what we currently  have which is admittedly a little on the  thin and so for some of the stuff that  we're doing in the future as I mentioned  we did saving being a synchronous is  probably one of the more difficult  things to work with I acknowledge with  the biggest problem just being how do I  know when it's even done so we added  interesting the line wrapping so we  added a current value that will actually  tell you the status of the queue this  will base  you can query the system to find out if  there's any tasks running in general you  can find out specifically if there's any  open tasks or any particular save tasks  and then you can also filter this not  only on you know as globally but also  for a particular notebook so for  instance kind of the idea behind this is  I have a an application that I've built  in a document that basically goes  through and generates a whole bunch of  other things and it's putting all those  other things up to the cloud you can  actually have you I in your document  that actually indicates you know hey I'm  generating these and now I'm saving them  off in the background and 